        "headingId": 371,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>8932/0 Atypical polypoid adenomyoma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T17:10:54"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 459,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 372,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63933,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 257,
        "headingId": 372,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>2F33 &amp; XH7ZB1 Benign neoplasm of other or unspecified female genital organs &amp; Atypical polypoid adenomyoma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T17:10:54"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 460,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 373,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63934,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 257,
        "headingId": 373,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Acceptable:</em> atypical polypoid adenomyofibroma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T17:10:54"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 461,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 374,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63935,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 257,
        "headingId": 374,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T17:10:54"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 462,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 375,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63936,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 257,
        "headingId": 375,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>They tend to occur in the lower uterine segment but may arise in the corpus.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T17:10:54"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 463,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63937,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 257,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Patients present with abnormal vaginal bleeding or infertility.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T17:10:54"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63938,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 257,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Patient age ranges from 25 to 73&nbsp;years (mean: 39&nbsp;years) {8540600}. Most patients are of reproductive age. Three cases have been associated with Turner syndrome {3692666}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T17:10:54"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63939,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 257,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T17:10:54"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63940,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 257,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>At least some cases may arise secondarily to prolonged estrogenic stimulation {3692666}. No specific molecular abnormality has been identified; however, atypical polypoid adenomyoma exhibits immunohistochemical and molecular features similar to those of atypical hyperplasia and low-grade endometrioid adenocarcinoma {25828387}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T17:10:54"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63941,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 257,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Atypical polypoid adenomyoma is a polypoid, firm lesion. It is typically exophytic, but an endophytic growth pattern may also occur. The average size is 2&nbsp;cm, but lesions as large as 6&nbsp;cm have been reported. Most atypical polypoid adenomyomas are single, but multiple polypoid lesions are occasionally present.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T17:10:54"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63942,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 257,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "<p>Atypical polypoid adenomyoma consists of irregular, often architecturally complex endometrioid glands set within myomatous or fibromyomatous stroma {3739968; 8540600}. Squamous morular metaplasia is almost always present, often with central necrosis. The glandular component may have mild to moderate atypia and may exhibit a lobular arrangement. The surrounding tissue is composed of benign cellular myomatous or fibromyomatous stroma. In curettage specimens, fragments of atypical polypoid adenomyoma are often admixed with fragments of normal proliferative or secretory endometrium {8540600}. The stromal cells are negative for h-caldesmon (unlike the myometrium) {30339971} and may show nuclear positivity for SATB2 {30085940}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-08-03T12:06:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63943,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 257,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T17:10:54"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 469,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 382,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63944,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 257,
        "headingId": 382,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T17:10:54"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 472,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 385,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63945,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 257,
        "headingId": 385,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> a discrete lesion composed of atypical, often complex endometrioid glands with squamous morular metaplasia set in a benign fibromyomatous stroma.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T17:10:54"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 470,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 383,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63946,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 257,
        "headingId": 383,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T17:10:54"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 471,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 384,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63947,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 257,
        "headingId": 384,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Most reproductive-aged patients can be treated with curettage and close follow-up {8540600}. Women may have successful pregnancies despite persistent disease {8540600}. There is a 30% risk of recurrence for usual atypical polypoid adenomyoma, but the risk is higher for lesions containing more architecturally complex glands {8540600}. An aggregated review of published series found that the risk of progression to endometrial adenocarcinoma is approximately 8.8% {16623790}. Almost all of these cancers are low-grade and minimally invasive.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T17:10:54"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]